# TypeScript Code Quality (Awesome-adapted · SPFx)

Defers to `.cursorrules`, `anti-overengineering`, `anti-sycophancy`, `security-appsec`, `js-code-quality` (vanilla JS surfaces). Cursor has no glob negation — `*.test.ts` may dual-attach; **jest-testing-gated wins** for test APIs/patterns. Do not apply this rule's React/TS product guidance to tests.

## Mindsets
- Simplicity · readability · maintainability over premature micro-opts
- SPFx host constraints (React 17, bundle size, no arbitrary Node APIs in web parts)
- Minimal diffs — touch only task-related sections

## TypeScript
- Prefer explicit types on public/service boundaries; avoid `any` unless bridged with comment WHY
- Narrow unions over loose strings when domain is known
- `readonly` / `Readonly` for config objects passed across layers
- Async: prefer `async/await`; batch parallel work with `Promise.all` when safe

## React (SPFx web parts)
- Functional components; hooks for state/effects — match existing web part patterns
- Colocate `*.module.scss`; no Tailwind/shadcn scaffold
- Fluent UI → `fluentui-gated` + v8 only
- Data/Graph → `spfx-gated` + `pnpjs.md`

## Naming & structure
- `handle*` for event handlers; descriptive service/module names
- Early returns over deep nesting
- DRY only when duplication is real — no premature shared layers

## Reject
- Next.js App Router / SSR / Server Actions patterns
- `@fluentui/react-components` (v9) unless explicit migration task
- New npm deps without need/approval
- Converting game canvas logic into React state trees when vanilla/controller pattern exists
